WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:10.072
It had been a long, unsuccessful night, and 18-year-old Elvis Presley was frustrated.

2
00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:15.914
This session was his big chance to break into the recording business, but no matter how hard he

3
00:00:15.995 --> 00:00:18.416
tried, he couldn't please the studio owner.

4
00:00:19.496 --> 00:00:20.416
Everyone took a break.

5
00:00:21.177 --> 00:00:26.219
Elvis sang one of his favorite songs to relax, and the guitar and bass players played along for

6
00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:33.000
fun. When the studio owner heard them, he knew this fast-paced, energetic sound was exactly what he wanted!

7
00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:38.345
Elvis had been trying too hard to imitate big singers of the day, when all he needed to

8
00:00:38.385 --> 00:00:39.446
do was be himself.

9
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:45.452
By being true to the music he loved, Elvis Presley would eventually become the king of rock and

10
00:00:45.472 --> 00:00:53.240
roll. Elvis was born on January 8, 1935, in a humble two-room house in Tupelo, Mississippi.

11
00:00:54.016 --> 00:00:59.280
His father, Vernon Presley, struggled to find work and was jailed for eight months for forging a check.

12
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.703
Afterward, the family had an even harder time making ends meet and had to move often.

13
00:01:05.544 --> 00:01:12.389
Between the frequent moves, an overprotective mother, and his father's shameful prison record, Elvis always felt different.

14
00:01:13.129 --> 00:01:15.050
He was a shy boy who had few friends.

15
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:20.114
"All I looked forward to was Sunday, when we could all go to church," he later said.

16
00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:24.100
There, he could do what he loved: sing.

17
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.923
When his parents bought him a guitar for his eleventh birthday, he learned to accompany himself when he

18
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:34.946
sang. In 1948, the family moved to Memphis, and Elvis' life slowly improved.

19
00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:37.807
Vernon found a job driving delivery trucks.

20
00:01:38.728 --> 00:01:41.730
Elvis started to enjoy the attention he received for being different.

21
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.992
Most boys had short hair, but Elvis wore his hair long "because that was the way the truck

22
00:01:47.032 --> 00:01:47.753
drivers wore it."

23
00:01:48.553 --> 00:01:53.536
Elvis also attracted attention because he dressed in the fancy clothes favored by African Americans.

24
00:01:54.460 --> 00:02:00.004
After he won his high school's talent contest, in which he sang and played guitar, Elvis finally gained

25
00:02:00.044 --> 00:02:01.445
some acceptance from his peers.

26
00:02:01.465 --> 00:02:05.328
"It was amazing how popular I became after that," he said.

27
00:02:05.868 --> 00:02:08.910
Best of all for Elvis, Memphis was a musical city.

28
00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:14.294
He loved the gospel music he heard in his church, as well as the gospel music of African

29
00:02:14.314 --> 00:02:15.155
American churches.

30
00:02:16.015 --> 00:02:20.598
He listened to country tunes by white performers on the radio and to the rhythm and blues played

31
00:02:20.638 --> 00:02:22.540
by local African American musicians.

32
00:02:23.612 --> 00:02:27.735
Elvis' rise to fame and fortune began just after he graduated from high school.

33
00:02:28.536 --> 00:02:34.401
He paid four dollars to make a vocal recording at the small Sun Records studio—supposedly as a birthday

34
00:02:34.421 --> 00:02:35.221
gift for his mother.

35
00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:40.626
The studio's owner liked what he heard and eventually matched Elvis up with a bass player and a

36
00:02:40.666 --> 00:02:48.432
guitarist. From 1954 to 1955, the group recorded five singles and performed all over the South.

37
00:02:49.256 --> 00:02:55.401
Elvis named their fusion of country, gospel, pop, and blues "rockabilly," but the rest of the world called

38
00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:56.382
it "rock and roll."

39
00:02:57.303 --> 00:03:03.008
In August 1955, Elvis signed on with "Colonel" Tom Parker, a country-music promoter.

40
00:03:03.788 --> 00:03:09.773
Within a year, the Colonel had gotten Elvis a recording contract with RCA, a big record company, as

41
00:03:09.853 --> 00:03:11.174
well as a movie contract.

42
00:03:12.055 --> 00:03:17.139
Maybe it was nerves, or that he needed to keep time to the fast beat, but whenever Elvis

43
00:03:17.179 --> 00:03:21.275
sang, he shook his hips in a way that made young audiences go wild.

44
00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:26.218
For this, Elvis got the nickname "Elvis the Pelvis," which he hated.

45
00:03:26.778 --> 00:03:29.180
He couldn't go anywhere without attracting fans.

46
00:03:30.101 --> 00:03:33.703
After one show, girls mobbed him and tore his clothing to shreds.

47
00:03:34.584 --> 00:03:37.926
Not everybody liked Elvis' uninhibited performances, however.

48
00:03:38.647 --> 00:03:40.388
Older people called Elvis vulgar.

49
00:03:41.268 --> 00:03:46.312
Of course, that made teenagers love him more—because to them, Elvis symbolized rebellion.

50
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:51.281
One fan recalled, "Hearing him for the first time was like busting out of jail."

51
00:03:52.202 --> 00:03:59.584
On his last appearance on Ed Sullivan's weekly TV show, in January 1957, network censors ordered cameramen to

52
00:03:59.644 --> 00:04:02.645
avoid Elvis' hips; they shot him from the waist up.

53
00:04:03.545 --> 00:04:08.947
At the same time, Ed Sullivan calmed fears that Elvis was a bad influence when the respected host

54
00:04:09.007 --> 00:04:12.348
told America that Elvis was "a real decent, fine boy."

55
00:04:13.256 --> 00:04:17.757
Elvis received fifty thousand dollars for his appearances on the show, a record-setting fee.

56
00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:24.979
This sum, plus the income from his hit records and other appearances, enabled Elvis to buy Graceland, an

57
00:04:25.019 --> 00:04:26.560
18-room mansion in Memphis.

58
00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:32.541
Elvis seemed unstoppable, with his music and movies making him an international star.

59
00:04:33.302 --> 00:04:38.403
But the United States Army interrupted his career when they drafted him in December 1957.

60
00:04:39.183 --> 00:04:44.401
By the time Elvis left in the spring of 1958, he had recorded enough songs to stay in

61
00:04:44.421 --> 00:04:45.702
the news while in the service.

62
00:04:46.502 --> 00:04:50.905
In the army, he refused any special treatment and served with a unit based in Germany.

63
00:04:51.705 --> 00:04:54.306
In 1960, Elvis was honorably discharged.

64
00:04:55.147 --> 00:05:01.230
Focusing on his movie career instead of touring, he became a wholesome, patriotic package that both teens and

65
00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.811
parents could like—and an even bigger star.

66
00:05:04.712 --> 00:05:11.335
In his movies, Elvis always appeared likeable, carefree, and graceful, and soundtracks released for the films kept Elvis'

67
00:05:11.375 --> 00:05:12.276
songs on the radio.

68
00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.402
Critics, however, claimed Elvis sold out by starring in movies that quickly made huge sums of money but

69
00:05:18.462 --> 00:05:19.623
lacked artistic value.

70
00:05:20.543 --> 00:05:27.306
Some fans claim Colonel Parker exploited Elvis, turning down excellent parts in serious films and refusing to pay

71
00:05:27.366 --> 00:05:30.028
for good songwriters, in order to make a bigger profit.

72
00:05:30.828 --> 00:05:34.390
Elvis later admitted, "I sure lost my musical direction in Hollywood.

73
00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:39.372
My songs were the same conveyor belt mass production, just like most of my movies."

74
00:05:40.592 --> 00:05:46.296
In 1968, in order to resurrect his sagging career, Elvis did a TV special in which he sang

75
00:05:46.316 --> 00:05:47.356
before a live audience.

76
00:05:48.277 --> 00:05:54.040
His performance revealed intensity, humor, emotion, and confidence—reminding people why they loved him.

77
00:05:54.981 --> 00:06:00.444
One music critic later wrote, "There is something magical about watching a man who has lost himself find

78
00:06:00.464 --> 00:06:01.285
his way back home."

79
00:06:02.245 --> 00:06:05.667
Elvis left Hollywood and vowed not to record songs he didn't believe in.

80
00:06:07.148 --> 00:06:12.591
Instead, he began a series of record-setting live performances in Las Vegas and other concerts across the United

81
00:06:12.631 --> 00:06:16.554
States, remaining a box-office sensation through the mid 1970s.

82
00:06:17.394 --> 00:06:22.417
But the work was exhausting, and Elvis took prescription drugs to pep him up before shows and to

83
00:06:22.458 --> 00:06:23.598
help him sleep afterward.

84
00:06:24.499 --> 00:06:29.942
He became addicted, and the heavy drug use hurt his body; his weight went up and down, and

85
00:06:29.982 --> 00:06:32.604
his behavior changed as his moods swung violently.

86
00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:40.146
His sudden death in his Graceland home on August 16, 1977, was attributed to a heart attack following

87
00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:41.467
a lethal mix of drugs.

88
00:06:42.428 --> 00:06:45.731
Since his death, Elvis Presley's popularity has only increased.

89
00:06:46.551 --> 00:06:51.996
Graceland opened to the public in 1982 and has become one of the most frequented tourist sites in

90
00:06:52.016 --> 00:06:52.817
the United States.

91
00:06:53.758 --> 00:06:58.582
A series of mysterious Elvis sightings started rumors that he had faked his own death to escape the

92
00:06:58.642 --> 00:07:00.604
pressures of stardom and was still alive.

93
00:07:01.536 --> 00:07:07.459
These were probably sightings of Elvis impersonators, who had begun imitating the 1970s version of the king of

94
00:07:07.519 --> 00:07:10.881
rock and roll and continue to attract audiences across the globe.

95
00:07:11.682 --> 00:07:17.145
While it's unlikely Elvis is still physically alive, he does live on in the hearts of people everywhere.
